A||N||3||WisconsinGT||Paint question's||||
Z||000000||WisconsinGT||09-08-1999||03:40 PM||||I am thinking about painting my car. While getting estimates, people have been saying that if they repaint the car, the spiderwebbing that is coming through the paint will come back in time. Is it true? Do I have to get a new front end? Also, there is a very slight wavey pattern on top of the car. That too, will come back in time. So they say.||||reg||
Z||000001||Cooter||09-08-1999||07:48 PM||||If you are talking about the bumper covers having some paint defects coming back, you would probably be right. But, if the paint on the car now has cracks or other problems, it needs to be stripped down to the factory sealer or primer before it gets refinished. Just painting over damaged paint hides problems for a short time, but to fix the paint so you dont have future surface defects, all the old paint needs to be removed. Because the Fiero is so small, this is not a problem, but I hate these jobs on big cars like the Sedan deVille or Continental. I usually begin with a 180 or 240 grit sandpaper on an orbital sander. Then fix any damage and primer the entire surface with high build urethane primer. This is then block sanded to find any more areas that need repair with 240 grit sandpaper and re-primered and then wet sanded with 400 grit before painting. If the messed up paint is fairly thin, I will just wet sand it off with 240 and then primer it to prep the car for painting.||||reg||
Z||000002||theogre||09-08-1999||08:52 PM||||part of the problem with the nose is the fact it's often more flexible than the paint. Chose paints carefully. Make sure the maker says it's ok, or that there are/aren't special instructions, for use on the surface you want to paint.||||reg||
Z||000003||lowCG||09-09-1999||12:38 AM||||Is it better to keep the paint as thin as possible(especially primer)to help keep the cracks away when painting over the urethane panels?||||reg||
